        "summaries": {
          "standar": "<h4>1. Tabel Komparasi Fundamental: Will vs Be Going To</h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Aspek Komparasi</th><th>Modal 'Will'</th><th>Frasa 'Be Going To'</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Karakteristik Keputusan</strong></td><td>Keputusan spontan saat berbicara tanpa rencana sebelumnya (<em>Spontaneous decision</em>).</td><td>Rencana atau niat yang telah dirancang sebelumnya (<em>Prior plan / arrangement</em>).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Dasar Prediksi</strong></td><td>Prediksi berdasarkan opini pribadi, harapan, atau intuisi subjektif.</td><td>Prediksi berdasarkan bukti nyata yang tampak jelas di depan mata (<em>Present evidence</em>).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Contoh Keputusan</strong></td><td><em>\"The server alarm is buzzing. I will check the CPU load right now.\"</em></td><td><em>\"I am going to submit my algorithm term paper tomorrow morning at 08:00 AM.\"</em></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Contoh Prediksi</strong></td><td><em>\"I believe AI will assist all developers by 2030.\"</em></td><td><em>\"Look at that memory leak graph! The server is going to crash in a few seconds.\"</em></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h4>2. Pola Rumus Gramatikal</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Formula Will:</strong> <code>Subject + will + Verb 1 murni</code> (Negatif: <code>will not / won't</code>)</li>\n  <li><strong>Formula Be Going To:</strong> <code>Subject + (am/is/are) + going to + Verb 1 murni</code></li>\n</ul>",
          "ringkas": "<div class=\"alert alert-success\" style=\"margin-bottom: 14px;\"><strong>⚡ INTISARI KILAT & POIN KUNCI UJIAN (CHEATSHEET) • PERTEMUAN 4</strong><br>Poin-poin konsep esensial, definisi baku, dan rangkuman hafalan cepat untuk persiapan kuis & UTS. Cocok untuk review kilat 5 menit!</div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>No</th><th>Topik Pembelajaran</th><th>Poin Kunci & Kaidah Mutlak yang Wajib Diingat</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>Three Modalities of Future Expression in Tech Projects</strong></td><td>Kuasai prinsip dasar, cermati istilah teknis baku, dan pahami alur penerapannya dalam kasus nyata perkuliahan.</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>Modal Auxiliaries for System Requirements (RFC 2119 Standard)</strong></td><td>Kuasai prinsip dasar, cermati istilah teknis baku, dan pahami alur penerapannya dalam kasus nyata perkuliahan.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card-dark\" style=\"margin-top: 12px;\"><strong style=\"color: #38bdf8;\">🎯 Tips Belajar & Rujukan Utama:</strong> Fokuskan pada penguasaan tabel perbandingan dan kerangka first principles. Rujukan: <em>Bradner, S. (1997). Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels (RFC 2119). IETF., Eastwood, John. (2008). Oxford Practice Grammar with Answers. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</em>.</div>",
          "detail": "<div class=\"alert alert-info\" style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>🤖 PANDUAN GURU AI (MASTER TEACHING GUIDE) • PERTEMUAN 4</strong><br>Sintesis komprehensif materi dosen, catatan praktikum mahasiswa, analisis first principles, bedah jebakan UTS, dan literatur standar dunia.</div>\n<h4>BAB 1: Three Modalities of Future Expression in Tech Projects</h4>\n\n<p>Professional technical communication demands clarity when stating future schedules, system predictions, and immediate operational commitments:</p>\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Future Structure</th>\n      <th>Syntactic Pattern</th>\n      <th>Degree of Intention & Evidence</th>\n      <th>Technical Engineering Context</th>\n      <th>Concrete Professional Example</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Will + V1</strong></td>\n      <td><code>Subject + will + Verb 1</code></td>\n      <td>Instant decision made at the moment of speaking, or formal contract commitment.</td>\n      <td>Live incident response during on-call triage or code review standup.</td>\n      <td><em>\"I see the server error alert; I <strong>will inspect</strong> the logs immediately.\"</em></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Be going to + V1</strong></td>\n      <td><code>Subject + am/is/are + going to + V1</code></td>\n      <td>Premeditated plan, or strong projection based on visible real-time empirical evidence.</td>\n      <td>Sprint roadmap milestones or telemetry dashboards warning of impending limits.</td>\n      <td><em>\"Look at the memory graph reaching 98%; the container <strong>is going to run out of memory</strong>!\"</em></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Present Continuous for Future</strong></td>\n      <td><code>Subject + am/is/are + V-ing</code></td>\n      <td>Fixed arrangement with external stakeholders at a confirmed future time slot.</td>\n      <td>Maintenance downtime windows, client feature demos, cloud migration schedules.</td>\n      <td><em>\"Our DevOps team <strong>is migrating</strong> the database to AWS RDS next Saturday at midnight.\"</em></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>BAB 2: Modal Auxiliaries for System Requirements (RFC 2119 Standard)</h4>\n\n<p>In standard engineering documentation (such as IETF RFCs and ISO software specifications), modal auxiliaries carry strictly defined legal meanings:</p>\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>RFC Keyword</th>\n      <th>Modal Verb</th>\n      <th>Architectural Requirement Level</th>\n      <th>System Specification Example</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>MUST / REQUIRED</strong></td>\n      <td><code>must / have to</code></td>\n      <td>Absolute requirement; violation breaks compatibility or security.</td>\n      <td><em>\"All passwords <strong>must</strong> be hashed using bcrypt with work factor &ge; 12.\"</em></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SHOULD / RECOMMENDED</strong></td>\n      <td><code>should / ought to</code></td>\n      <td>Strong recommendation; valid reasons may exist in particular cases to ignore it, but full implications must be weighed.</td>\n      <td><em>\"The backend API <strong>should</strong> implement rate-limiting on login endpoints.\"</em></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>MAY / OPTIONAL</strong></td>\n      <td><code>may / can</code></td>\n      <td>Truly optional feature; implementations must interoperate whether present or not.</td>\n      <td><em>\"The client application <strong>may</strong> cache static assets in localStorage for offline access.\"</em></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"card\" style=\"margin-top: 14px;\"><strong>📚 Referensi Literatur Akademik & Standar Industri:</strong><ul style=\"font-size: 0.85em; margin-top: 4px;\"><li>Bradner, S. (1997). Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels (RFC 2119). IETF.</li><li>Eastwood, John. (2008). Oxford Practice Grammar with Answers. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</li></ul></div>"
        },
